  READ THE SPREAD COLUMN AS THE ACCURACY THE EXPERIMENT NEEDS. A counter good
  to a few percent separates these with an order of magnitude to spare, which
  is why the cell is worth renting a box for and why a one-launch probe is not.

THE COMMAND, on a box where --probe says OPEN.

  # 1. the timed ladder, unprofiled, so time and traffic come from the same cell
  python scripts/block_m_crossing_sweep.py --model mixtral-8x7b --dtype bf16 \
      --tiles 32 --group-m 16 --block-n 64 \
      --r-max 256 --row-step 32 --step-probes 0

  # 2. the same cell under the counter, one tile count per invocation.
  #    The instrument (moe/bench/timing.time_kernel) warms up for a DURATION
  #    of sustained load, --warmup in MILLISECONDS, and sizes its own
  #    iteration count from that load: there is no --iters any more (the
  #    flag is retired and ignored) and a zero warmup is REFUSED outright
  #    (timing.warm_until). So the profiled launch count is never one; it is
  #    warmup_calls + iters x trials fused_experts calls of about five kernels
  #    each, plus one L2-flush kernel per timed iteration. The smallest
  #    settings the instrument accepts keep that small (1 ms of warmup is one
  #    call of a millisecond-scale kernel; iters floors at 10; one trial), and
  #    the cell row the sweep writes carries the iters and trials it used.
  for n in 1 2 3 4 6 8; do
    ncu --metrics dram__bytes_read.sum,dram__bytes_write.sum,lts__t_sector_op_read_hit_rate.pct,gpu__time_duration.sum \
        --replay-mode kernel --cache-control all \
        --csv --page raw --target-processes all \
        --log-file counters-n$n-ccall.csv \
        python scripts/block_m_crossing_sweep.py --model mixtral-8x7b \
          --tiles 32 --group-m 16 --block-n 64 \
          --r-max $(( n * 32 )) --row-step $(( n * 32 )) --step-probes 0 \
          --warmup 1 --trials 1 --cell-budget-ms 1
  done

  # 3. reduce, then score. Sum every byte metric PER KERNEL NAME over all
  #    profiled launches and divide by the ACTUAL call count: `calls` is the
  #    number of moe_align_block_size launches in the CSV (exactly one per
  #    fused_experts call; fused_moe_kernel runs twice per call, up and down),
  #    and it must equal the row's iters x trials plus the warmup's calls, so
  #    REFUSE a CSV whose count is not above iters x trials. Write the per-call
  #    figures as dram_bytes_read etc. and the count as `calls`. Never divide
  #    by an assumed 1: the instrument decides the count, the row records it.
  python scripts/dram_counter_route.py --analyse counters.json

CACHE CONTROL, and why it is a swept parameter rather than a default.
  ncu's default is Flush All: all GPU caches are flushed before each replay
  iteration. That is a DIFFERENT cache state from the timed sweep, which never
  flushes. The LEVEL it can move is bounded by L2, and L2 is 40-60 MB against a
  2.82 GB weight read -- at most 2.1% of R(1). The SLOPE,
  which is the claim, differences that term away entirely. Run both modes
  anyway: two agreeing numbers close the question, and two disagreeing ones
  are themselves the result.

COST.
  6 tile counts x 2 cache modes = 12 profiled invocations of at least 11 fused_experts calls each,
  about 660 profiled kernel launches plus 120 L2-flush launches.
  The largest cell is n=8 (256 rows per expert), which the timed
  sweep measures in single-digit milliseconds; ncu replay and its save/restore
  of the 2.8 GB weight buffers dominate, at seconds per launch. Budget
  an hour of GPU time and two pod-hours end to end, not the fifteen minutes
  the one-launch recipe used to promise. At A100 spot rates that is still a
  few dollars. The cost of this experiment has never been the money.

WHICH KERNELS TO KEEP, and why the profile is NOT name-filtered.
  All of W moves inside the two `fused_moe_kernel` launches; the auxiliary
  launches (align, reduction) move activation-sized traffic only. Filtering to
  the GEMM would still be defensible -- but then the measured level would no
  longer be comparable to the byte model, which charges the whole layer. So
  profile everything, record bytes PER KERNEL NAME, and let the analysis
  report both totals. A filter applied at capture cannot be undone afterwards.

SCHEMA the run must write, so --analyse can score it:
{
  "device": "nvidia_a100_sxm4_80gb",       # the card slug, matching the calibration file
  "model": "mixtral-8x7b", "dtype": "bf16",
  "group_m": 16, "block_n": 64, "block_k": 64,
  "num_warps": 8, "num_stages": 3, "block_m": 32,
  "cache_control": "all",           # ncu --cache-control; "all" or "none"
  "ridge": 145.81,                # THIS card's own calibration, never 160.3
  "anchors":  {"published": 0.6473, "t1": 0.4522, "n3": 0.7047},
  "bracket":  [0.4522, 0.6313],
  "rows": [ {"n": 1, "calls": 11, "launches": 55, "dram_bytes_read": 2.84e9,
             "dram_bytes_write": 1.1e8, "l2_read_hit_pct": 4.2,
             "gpu_time_ns": 1.95e6,
             "by_kernel": {"fused_moe_kernel": 2.81e9, "moe_sum": 3.0e7} }, ... ]
}

  Every key is required. `--analyse` raises on a missing one rather than
  defaulting it: a zero that was never measured is the failure mode this whole
  file exists to avoid. `calls` is the number of fused_experts calls the
  instrument made (warmup + iters x trials), counted from the profile's own
  launch list; `launches` is every profiled kernel launch; the byte fields are
  PER CALL, the sums over all launches divided by `calls`, never by an
  assumed one.

